==============================================================================
3. ★ Provider-level displacement -- does the operator come back?
==============================================================================
  A takedown removes a HOST. The resilience question is whether the
  operator reappears as a NEW host under the SAME provider suffix.
  (Registrable suffix, or /24 for IP literals.)

     W  removals    new-host        rate
     1     33279        2274       6.83%
     3     32279        3758      11.64%
     7     30688        5933      19.33%
    30     22072        7354      33.32%

  ★ Compare with the host-level table in section 2: if the provider
  rate is close to the host rate, the 'new URL' is usually a new HOST
  (displacement); if it is much lower, the same host is being reused.
  provider-level rate at W=7 uses 30688 removals.

==============================================================================
4. Placebo for the provider-level measure
==============================================================================
  real provider displacement (W=7d) = 19.33%
  placebo (removal times shuffled)  = 22.38% (sd 0.19), p = 1.0000
  ★ As with the host measure, the placebo floor is NOT zero -- a
  provider with many hosts collects a hit by chance. The effect size is
  the EXCESS over the placebo, -3.05 pp, not the raw rate.

